adjudication.rationale:
In `create_account_controller.js`, any failed signup response that includes `result.errors.email` causes `result.values.email` to be pushed into `rejectedEmails` (no check for a specific error like “blocked” vs “taken”):
```js
if (result.errors && result.errors.email && result.values) {
  createAccountController.get('rejectedEmails').pushObject(result.values.email);
}
```
Later, `emailValidation` short-circuits and marks the email as invalid purely based on membership in `rejectedEmails`, returning a generic `user.email.invalid` reason:
```js
if (this.get('rejectedEmails').contains(email)) {
  return Discourse.InputValidation.create({ failed: true, reason: I18n.t('user.email.invalid') });
}
```
So an email rejected for *any* server-side reason (including uniqueness “has already been taken”, whitelist/blacklist, etc.) becomes permanently client-rejected for that exact value for the life of the controller/modal, with generic “invalid” messaging. This matches the finding’s core claim of conflation and persistent invalid state until reload/close.
